Settings_InsteadPassingGameS_Fmt:
Instead of passing this game's own force feedback through, the plugin can build the steering force itself. There's a real sense of grip. The wheel goes light as the front washes out and it pulls into a countersteer as the rear steps out.

It also unlocks your wheel's rev lights: they share a channel with game force feedback, so they can only run when the plugin owns the whole signal, as it does here.

To try it, set this game's force feedback and vibration to 0 in its wheel settings, so the plugin is the only force on the wheel.{0}

Settings_PluginCanCarryLe_Fmt:
The plugin can carry Le Mans Ultimate's force feedback for you. It does not replace it: the sim still works out what the car is doing and hands over those same forces, so the feel stays the sim's own.

What it buys you is your wheel's rev lights and screen. They share a channel with force feedback, so they can only run when the plugin owns that channel instead of the sim.

It needs two things on the game's side first: Vendor Specific Force Feedback off (Settings > Controls > Force Feedback) and Use LEDs off (Settings > Wheel and Pedals > Calibration). Its force feedback strength can stay. Start SimHub before the game, or the lights and screen may not come on.{0}

Settings_PluginCanCarryRaceRoom_Fmt:
The plugin can carry RaceRoom's force feedback for you. It does not replace it: RaceRoom still works out what the car is doing and hands over those same forces, so the feel stays the sim's own.

What it buys you is your wheel's rev lights and screen. They share a channel with force feedback, so they can only run when the plugin owns that channel instead of the sim.

It needs one thing on RaceRoom's side first: disable the game's own force feedback, whatever its intensity slider says. Start SimHub before RaceRoom, or the lights and screen may not come on.{0}
